test(visual): screenshots showed the previous frame about a quarter of the time

capturePage() hands back the last frame the compositor produced, which is not
necessarily one that reflects the DOM as it stands. MEASURED by flipping a
full-window background colour and capturing immediately: 8 of 30 captures came
back showing the PREVIOUS colour with the window focused, and 8 of 30 with
another window on top - about 27% either way, and every wrong frame was exactly
the previous one rather than a blend, so these are whole stale frames rather
than tearing. Awaiting two animation frames first drove it to 0 of 60.

This matters more here than it would elsewhere: reviewing screenshots is a
primary verification step in this project and several defects were found by
looking at one. The artifact is freshly written and looks entirely correct, so
a stale frame is a confident wrong answer - the same disease as the stale
artifact captureScreenshot already guards against, wearing a different coat.

Two things settleFrame() deliberately does NOT do, both decided by measurement
rather than argument:

- it does not raise or focus the window. showInactive + moveTop + focus + 350ms
  was the recipe carried in from an earlier investigation; measured against the
  double rAF it fixed nothing further (0 of 12 either way) while costing 350ms
  per shot and stealing focus from whatever the reader is doing - the same harm
  trapExternalOpens exists to prevent.

- it never awaits those frames unconditionally. On a hidden window
  requestAnimationFrame never fires at all (measured: still pending after 2s),
  so an unraced await would hang a suite forever inside a function whose whole
  contract is that it cannot take a run down with it.

The oracle reads the PNG back rather than trusting the capture call, because
what is defended is the content of the file a human ends up looking at.

THE PAGE SHAPE IN THE ORACLE IS LOAD-BEARING. The first version used a 400x300
solid colour and R256 came back VACUOUS - a trivial page composites faster than
the capture round-trip, so it is never stale and the assertion could not fail.
Measured stale rates with the settle removed, 24 samples each: 400x300 plain
0/24, 1200x900 plain 1/24, 1200x900 blurred 15/24, 1200x900 blurred over a
re-rastering layer 23/24. The last is what ships; anything cheaper turns the
assertion back into decoration.

test:visual 15 -> 18. R256 PROVEN at exactly one assertion (23 of 24 stale with
the settle removed); 72 consecutive clean captures with it.

